  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Let $C$  be the set of all multiples of $10$ greater than $0$ and let $D$  be the the set of all multiples of $5$ greater than $0.$ What is $C\u2216D?$",
  "worked_solution": "The multiples of $10$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nC={10,20,30,40,50,60,70,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $5$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nD={5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,\u2026}.\n$$\n\nNow, $C\u2216D$ is the *difference* of $C$ and $D,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $C$ but *not* in $D.$\n\nThe elements of $C$ that are also in $D$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $10$). So, we must remove these elements from $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C\u2216D & =C-D \\\\  & ={10,20,30,40,50,60,70,\u2026}-{5,10,15,20,25,30,35,40,45,50,55,60,65,70,75,\u2026} \\\\  & =\u2205.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
